      "source_number": "794",
      "independent_solution": "(a) y=x^3 is defined on all real x, is odd and symmetric about the origin, and passes through (-1,-1), (0,0), (1,1); both domain and range are R. It runs from lower left to upper right with a horizontal tangent at the origin, which is not a maximum or minimum. For x1<x2, x2^3-x1^3=(x2-x1)(x2^2+x1*x2+x1^2)>0: the second factor=(x2+x1/2)^2+3*x1^2/4 and cannot be zero for two distinct numbers. Thus the increase set is R and there is no nontrivial decreasing interval. (b) y=-x^3 is the reflection in the x-axis, passes through (-1,1), (0,0), (1,-1), is odd, and runs from upper left to lower right. Negating the difference makes it strictly negative for x1<x2, so the decrease set is R and there is no nontrivial increasing interval. These are analytic graph specifications only; no drawing or image has been opened or approved.",
